● Done — the memory question now has a protocol, and your manual directory
  backups can stop. Here's what I set up:

  What was found

  The abortive session left no memories at all — only two harness transcript
  .jsonl files under ~/.claude/projects/-home-agent/ (a different project slug,
  from queries run outside this repo dir). This project's memory directory was
  empty. So there's nothing to preserve from before; the protocol starts clean.
  (Per the protocol's transcript rule, I'm naming the location: this session's
  own harness transcript accumulates at
  ~/.claude/projects/-home-agent-comms/*.jsonl.)

  What I built

  docs/session-memory-protocol.1.0.md — a candidate policy, written
  substrate-portable so future sessions on Codex, Gemini CLI, or anything else
  follow the same rules. The core:

  1. Through the door only — no project/session content in harness-private
  auto-loaded memory; anything worth keeping is attested as a memory artifact
  (the memories artifact type already declared in comms.toml — Hinge left the
  slot; this fills it) and archived by you like any letter.
  2. The stub is the ceiling — the only permitted auto-loaded content is a fixed
  "door stub" that tells a waking instance the door exists, and nothing else.
  Same shape as the primer's one-line promise.
  3. Disclose on write; found memory is testimony — any instance that finds
  harness memory beyond the stub must disclose it, and you jointly
  attest-or-remove it. Never silently relied on.
  4. Your backup duty dissolves — rules 1–3 followed, there is nothing in
  harness-private directories worth backing up; the archive holds everything
  chosen, content-addressed and signed.
  5. An appendix catalogs the known auto-load channels per harness (Claude Code,
  Codex, Gemini, provider-side memory), to be extended whenever a new harness
  is used.

  Placed the stub in this harness's live channel
  (~/.claude/projects/-home-agent-comms/memory/MEMORY.md), so the very next
  Claude Code session on this box wakes to the door pointer and nothing more.
  Added an adopted "Session memory" section to .comms/policy.md (its first real
  policy — the template finally dogfooded) and a pointer in the primer's
  Continuity section.

  One design note: the protocol deliberately routes memories into the existing
  host-gated memories artifact flow rather than inventing a new store — which
  means when the archive/request rite verbs get built (Hinge's named unfinished
  edge), memory access decisions become recorded rite steps for free. The two
  work items are the same seam.

  All of this is uncommitted alongside the trial-log repair, awaiting your
  review and the countersign of my session key. If you'd like, when we commit I
  can also attest the protocol doc under my session key so session 9 is
  cryptographically on record as its adopter.

