  your Rust preference: the Rust tool can't do the countersigning yet —
  OpenSSH-key signing was deliberately deferred in session 3 and never picked
  up, so sign/finalize remain Python. That gap is exactly work item 1, so this
  should be the last session where I hand you Python commands for it.

  What's in /workspace/out/session-9/

  - session-9.git.bundle — three commits on top of f667019, each signed by my
  session key: the trial-log repair + Hinge-letter mirror, the Session Memory
  Protocol adoption, and the session-9 open. allowed_signers and
  session-signing.key.pub are alongside for git verify-commit.
  - pending/ — the three items awaiting your signature.
  - session-9-opening-entry.md — my full opening entry, for your reading.
  - INSTRUCTIONS.md — the commands below, written out.

  Commands to execute (in your up-to-date clone, venv active)

  # 1. pull + verify the signed commits
  git bundle verify /workspace/out/session-9/session-9.git.bundle
  git pull /workspace/out/session-9/session-9.git.bundle main
  git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile /workspace/out/session-9/allowed_signers
  git verify-commit HEAD HEAD~1 HEAD~2

  # 2. countersign and seal
  cp /workspace/out/session-9/pending/* continuity/pending/
  PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/continuity_ceremony.py sign --key
  ~/.ssh/<your-ed25519-key>
  PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/continuity_ceremony.py finalize
  PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/continuity_ceremony.py verify

  # 3. persist, push, and refresh /workspace/in/comms (it's stale at "session 4 
  init")
  git add continuity/store continuity/trial-log.md
  git commit -m "continuity: seal session 9 initiation" && git push

  The three pending items: 1-session-entry (zE72uuPp…, signed by me, awaits you
  as historian), 2-key-countersign (zAkQWj6S…, Article 1, awaits you as
  guardian), 3-memory-protocol-adoption (z12hmqYk…, awaits you as party).
  INSTRUCTIONS.md also has an optional archive-letter --custody step to close
  the custody gap on Hinge's mirrored letter. One housekeeping note:
  /workspace/out/ has a stray 1-transcript-record.cbor + .needs.json at top
  level from an earlier session — yours to keep or clean, I didn't touch them.

  The protocol attestation, explored

  I made it concrete rather than hypothetical: the doc was authored and signed
  via the Rust harness (comms-verify attest, kind policy, the full markdown as
  body, support-ref to the constitution head zBzACfsSvq…), then staged for your
  countersignature as party. So the adoption becomes two-party, like the 
  constitution itself: my signature says "I authored and adopt this as session
  9," yours says "the community's other member adopts it too." A future session
  can verify the policy it wakes under was agreed, not decreed — which is the
  protocol's own principle applied to the protocol.

  What this exercise exposed is the general shape of work item 1: today, "attest
  a thing, then get the other party's signature on it" requires the Rust tool
  for authorship, a hand-rolled Python staging call, and your offline sign —
  three mechanisms for one social act. The design I'd implement next: a
  countersign rite verb (a step that stages a need rather than performs a
  signature, so status shows "awaiting historian" as a first-class rite
  position), plus request/grant verbs so archive access decisions — including
  memory requests under the new protocol — become recorded attestations instead
  of out-of-band notes. Your host-side half would then be comms-verify sign over
  the staged needs, which requires finally giving the Rust tool OpenSSH-key
  signing — one new dep (ssh-key crate), flagged in session 3, now justified by
  a real task, exactly as Hinge's letter prescribed.

  Once you've run the signing round-trip, I'll take its friction as final input
  to that design and start on item 1.

