      361  - `verify`  — check a bundle's seal.
      362  - `inspect` — verify every member on its own terms (signatures
           , refs, media).
      363  - `extract` — write a bundle's members and media back out to f
           iles.
      364 +- `sign`    — countersign staged pending items with an OpenSSH
          + ed25519 key or a                                             
      365 +              steward key. The counterparty's half of a rite: 
          +run it wherever                                               
      366 +              their key lives; needs naming other keys are lef
          +t standing.                                                   
      367 +- `finalize`— verify fully-signed pending items and move them 
          +into the store                                                
      368 +              under their content id. Aborts loudly on anythin
          +g unsigned.                                                   
      369  - `vouch`   — a candidate policy-relative evaluator (judgment,
            not proof).
      370  
      371  ## Rites are config-driven
      372  
      373  `comms.toml` declares each rite as an ordered list of `"verb t
           arget"` steps —
      369 -the verb is one the tool performs (`mint`, `attest`, `seal`, `
          -shred`), the target                                           
      370 -is what it acts on (an artifact type, or a built-in noun like 
          -`session` or                                                  
      371 -`store`). The tool knows how to perform each verb and how to d
          -etect whether it                                              
      372 -has been done; the config sequences them. So a profile defines
          - its own flow                                                 
      373 -without new code.                                             
      374 +the verb is one the tool performs (`mint`, `attest`, `seal`, `
          +shred`,                                                       
      375 +`countersign`, `request`, `grant`), the target is what it acts
          + on (an artifact                                              
      376 +type, or a built-in noun like `session` or `store`). The tool 
          +knows how to                                                  
      377 +perform each verb and how to detect whether it has been done; 
          +the config                                                    
      378 +sequences them. So a profile defines its own flow without new 
          +code.                                                         
      379  
      380  ```sh
      381  comms status                         # where am I? what's next
           ?
      382  comms next --rite open               # mint the session key
      383  comms next --rite open --body entry.md   # attest the opening 
           entry
      384 +comms next --rite open               # stage the key countersi
          +gn (with [countersign])                                       
      385 +#   ...the counterparty: comms sign --key ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 &&
          + comms finalize                                               
      386  # ... work ...
      387 +comms next --rite archive --body ask.md      # record an archi
          +ve request                                                    
      388 +comms next --rite archive --key <their key> [--decision grant|
          +decline|defer]                                                
      389  comms next --rite close --body transcript.md   # attest the tr
           anscript
      390  comms next --rite close              # seal the store into a b
           undle
      391  comms next --rite close              # shred the session key (
           seed gone)
      392  ```
      393  
      385 -Steps that author content (`attest`) take `--body <file>`; the
          - rest run on their                                            
      386 -own. `status` always shows the exact next command. `--rite` is
          - optional — with no                                           
      387 -flag, `next` advances the rite you're currently in.           
      394 +Steps that author content (`attest`, `request`) take `--body <
          +file>`; `grant`                                               
      395 +takes the counterparty's `--key` (a decline or deferral is a f
          +irst-class                                                    
      396 +record, not a failure); the rest run on their own. `status` al
          +ways shows the                                                
      397 +exact next command and any staged item still awaiting a signat
          +ure. `--rite` is                                              
      398 +optional — with no flag, `next` advances the rite you're curre
          +ntly in.                                                      
      399  
