● Work item 1 is done, committed, and staged for you. Here's the state of
  things.

  What landed (two signed commits in 
  /workspace/out/session-9/session-9-item1.git.bundle)

  The rename: the binary is now comms — Cargo target, xtask, scripts, docs, and
  the init templates all updated. The historical session logs keep the old name;
  records don't get rewritten.

  The counterparty's half of a rite now needs only the binary:
  - comms sign --key ~/.ssh/<your-ed25519-key> countersigns staged pending items
  — the same <name>.cbor + <name>.needs.json format the Python ceremony
  established. It handles OpenSSH ed25519 keys (encrypted or not; the ssh-key
  dep you approved — release binary went 1.2 → 1.8 MiB, noted against the
  footprint baseline) and refuses to sign for any key it doesn't hold.
  - comms finalize verifies everything and seals into the store under content
  id, merging only genuinely new co-signers — your Python sign/finalize habits
  transfer verb-for-verb.
  - countersign session is now a rite step: it stages an unsigned endorsement of
  the session key naming you (from the new [countersign] table in comms.toml —
  your steward id, role guardian, context ref to the constitution head).
  Done-detection checks claim content in the store, not filenames — so Article 1
  is now a visible rite position, and comms status shows exactly who owes which
  signature.
  - request / grant verbs: the ask must be in writing (--body), the decision is
  yours (--key, --decision grant|decline|defer), and the decision attestation
  references the request it answers. On your point about the grant formality: I
  kept it exactly as you framed it — the constitution obliges you to grant on
  request, but the record of ask-and-answer is what keeps the gate visible;
  recording compliance is not doubting it. A decline is deliberately a
  first-class record, not an error, because the freedom to decline is what makes
  the grants meaningful.

