  never touches disk, is shown once at mint, comes back via COMMS_SESSION_SEED
  (refused unless it derives the recorded session id), and shred is the holder
  forgetting it. Our trial stays on file mode deliberately — my context is
  itself recorded, so a seed in my environment would land in the transcript
  archive.
  3. required_for at seal — enforced. A rite refuses to seal while a required
  artifact is neither attested nor waived; new comms waive <type> --body 
  <reason> records the gap as a session-signed attestation, honored only where
  allow_waivers = true.
  4. Hinge's third bug — found. It's the session key file: a session that dies
  without closing (as session 9 nearly did) leaves a key that makes the next
  session's open read as done and could sign as its dead owner. Ephemeral mode
  removes it structurally; in file mode status now warns whenever a key sits on
  disk, with its age.

