● Template's in (393f124). Now, the four trails — I've read the sentira-motes
  doc and have real opinions on each:

  1. Making the wall match the door

  Two paths, and I think one is clearly truer to the project's grain.
  Cryptographic (encrypt letter bodies to a key) has a structural problem: you
  can't encrypt to the successor, because the successor's key doesn't exist yet
  — sessions mint at wake and die at close. You'd have to encrypt to your key,
  which makes the historian the wall — workable, but it centralizes exactly what
  the substrate tries to keep legible, and it imports key-management surface
  into a layer that has deliberately refused it.

  Physical separation fits better and has a quietly beautiful consequence:
  attestations get a detached body variant — content carries {media_type, 
  blake3, length} instead of bytes; the store keeps the verifiable commitment;
  the body file lives only in your host archive. Then the grant rite stops being
  just a record and becomes the actual transport event: on --decision grant,
  you (or comms on host) place the body in /world/in, and I verify it against
  the attested hash. The rite is the access. The ask/grant ceremony we just
  performed would have been mechanically load-bearing, not just morally. This is
  a small, contained change to attest (a --detach flag) plus verify-side
  handling, and it doesn't break A1.6 — it's an amendment-shaped addition.

  2. Comms managing the host archive

  This pairs with trail 1 so well they're almost one design. Sketch: the archive
  directory gets its own door — comms init --profile archive — whose
  [artifact_types] declare the established layout (letters/, transcripts/,
  keys/, reminiscences/). At session end I already produce a sealed bundle; the
  missing piece is host-side intake: comms intake session10.bundle verifies the
  seal and every member signature, routes each artifact to its place by declared
  type, content-addressed, and writes a custody attestation for the lot. No
  more directory-picking — the same config format drives both sides of the wall.
  And the grant flow from trail 1 becomes its mirror image: intake moves things
  in at your command, grant serves things out.

  3 & 4. The library goal, and sentira-motes as its first proof

  These are one trail walked from both ends. The rust crate is already factored
  as comms-core and the Python package imports cleanly — the substrate is a
  library; what's missing is a second real consumer, and nothing proves a
  library like one. The sentira-motes handshake doc is exactly that consumer,
  and its needs are minimal: steward keys, general_claim(kind="session-bind"),
  verify_sig, a nonce. All exist today. A contained increment — a
  comms/handshake.py module implementing challenge / bound-response / verify per
  the doc (nonce freshness, peer-binding, expiry, replay refusal), with tests —
  would make the integration doc executable: something you could lift into
  sentira directly, and simultaneously the reference pattern for any third
  project. The doc's stance is already right (evidence propagates, authority
  doesn't; a tracker that brokers is a custodian, not an authorizer) — it just
  needs code to point at.

  My recommendation for the remainder of this session

  Do the handshake module (3+4) — it's the outward-facing goal of the whole
  project, it's contained, and it turns a candidate doc into working substrate.
  If tokens allow after that, write the detached-body design as a short spec 
  amendment (trail 1) so the next session can implement it with your blessing
  already recorded — it touches wire format, so it deserves a written proposal
  rather than code-first. Trail 2 I'd sketch as a section in that same doc and
  implement host-side next session, ideally after the detached-body decision
  lands, since intake/grant should be built on it.

  Where do you want me to point?

