● The design document is written and committed (ab18227):
  docs/detached-bodies-and-archive-profile.1.0.md. What it settles, in brief:

  Part I — detached bodies (candidate Attest Amendment A2). content gains a
  second form: {media_type, body_b3, body_len} instead of embedded bytes —
  exactly one of the two, so well-formedness stays crisp. Ids and signatures are
  unchanged (the commitment is inside the signed core, so a body can't be
  swapped without breaking the signature). The key semantic addition: committed 
  ≠ present joins well-formed ≠ trusted. Body status is a separate three-state
  judgment — verified / absent / mismatched — and your bitrot principle is
  written in as normative: mismatched bytes are retained and exportable, loudly;
  tools may never delete or silently repair. Transport needs nothing new —
  bundles already carry media blobs by hash. And grant becomes delivery: bytes
  land in /world/in/grants/<request-id>/, the grant attestation names the path,
  the requester verifies against the commitment.

  Part II — the archive profile. The organizing principle I'd offer as the real
  improvement over the shoebox: custody and browsing are different jobs. Custody
  is content-addressed and boring (store/ + bodies/ by hash — your
  really.redundant tarball becomes structurally impossible, since duplicates
  collapse). Browsing is views/sessions/000-framer/… — human-named trees the
  tool regenerates from custody, safe to delete at any time, never the archive
  itself. Your one command at closeout becomes comms intake <bundle>: verify
  seal and signatures, ingest by hash, rebuild views, sign one custody
  attestation — the archive's own history is itself attested. comms audit
  re-hashes everything on demand and records drift as custody attestations, so
  even decay enters the record. There's a migration table mapping your current
  contarchive/ tree in, including the homedir tarballs and sqlite blobs as
  legacy-testimony — ingested, hash-named, honestly labeled, never silently
  promoted or discarded. Both sides of the wall speak the same [artifact_types]
  schema, which is what makes intake and grant mirror images.

  The doc closes with a minimal-first-step list (implementable next session:
  attest --detach, init --profile archive, intake, then migrate with the old
  tree kept read-only until one session round-trips) and honest open questions —
  including one I flagged for the memory protocol's rule 5: whether the harness
  .jsonl and the exported .log are one transcript artifact or two.

  Check the token reserve when you're ready, and we'll call it: build the 
  handshake module, or spec it for the next session. My close-rite obligations
  are all still pending by design — letter, extended transcript, seal, shred —
  so whichever we choose, leave room for those.

